The Anatomy of a CSS Rule

A CSS rule has two main parts: a **Selector** (which tells the browser WHICH element to style) and a **Declaration Block** (which tells the browser WHAT styling to apply).

```css
h1 {
  color: #2563eb;
  font-size: 32px;
}
```

Inside the curly braces `{ }`, you write `property: value;`. Notice the colon `:` between them and the semicolon `;` at the end of every line.

/* The World Famous Trick to Center Anything */
.center-box {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center; /* Centers horizontally */
  align-items: center;     /* Centers vertically */
  height: 100vh;           /* Full screen height */
}

/* Text color */
h1 {
  color: #1e293b;
}

/* Background color */
body {
  background-color: #f8fafc;
}

/* The Box Model in Action */
.my-box {
  width: 300px;
  padding: 20px;          /* Space INSIDE the border */
  border: 2px solid #000; /* The outline */
  margin: 30px;           /* Space OUTSIDE the border */
}

The Golden Rule for Perfect Sizing

By default, if you set width to 300px and add 20px padding on each side, the box grows to 340px! That surprise growth breaks many layouts.

To prevent this, web developers add this magic reset rule at the top of every stylesheet:

```css
* {
  box-sizing: border-box;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
}
```

`box-sizing: border-box` ensures that width: 300px means 300px total, keeping padding and borders neatly inside!
Remember: Padding is space inside the border; margin is space outside the border. box-sizing: border-box keeps sizes exact.

